{"task_id": "Mbpp/100", "solution": "def next_smallest_palindrome(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the next smallest palindrome strictly greater than the given integer n.\n    Handles negative inputs by treating them as their absolute value.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _int_to_digits(num_val: int) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Converts an integer to a list of its digits.\"\"\"\n        if num_val == 0:\n            return [0]\n        digits_list = []\n        # Process absolute value to handle potential negative inputs as per design\n        num_val = abs(num_val)\n        while num_val > 0:\n            digits_list.append(num_val % 10)\n            num_val //= 10\n        return digits_list[::-1]\n\n    def _digits_to_int(digits_list: list[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Converts a list of digits back to an integer.\"\"\"\n        num = 0\n        for digit in digits_list:\n            num = num * 10 + digit\n        return num\n\n    # Treat negative inputs as absolute value as per design\n    n_val = abs(n)\n    if n_val == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    digits = _int_to_digits(n_val)\n    num_digits = len(digits)\n\n    # Determine the length of the left part to mirror\n    # For odd num_digits, this includes the middle digit.\n    # For even num_digits, this is exactly half the digits.\n    half_len = (num_digits + 1) // 2\n    \n    # Extract the left part (including the middle digit if num_digits is odd)\n    left_part = digits[:half_len]\n\n    # Form an initial palindrome candidate by mirroring the left part\n    # The suffix is the reverse of the first `num_digits // 2` digits of the left part.\n    current_palindrome_digits = left_part + left_part[:num_digits // 2][::-1]\n    current_palindrome_int = _digits_to_int(current_palindrome_digits)\n\n    # If the current palindrome is already strictly greater than n_val, it's the answer.\n    # This handles cases like n=123 (palindrome 121 is not >123, so it continues)\n    # but also n=121 (palindrome 121 is not >121, so it continues)\n    # and n=191 (palindrome 191 is not >191, so it continues)\n    if current_palindrome_int > n_val:\n        return current_palindrome_int\n\n    # If the current palindrome is not greater (i.e., it's <= n_val),\n    # we need to increment the 'left_part' and try again.\n    \n    # Convert the left part to an integer and increment it\n    left_part_int = _digits_to_int(left_part)\n    left_part_int += 1\n    \n    # Convert the incremented left part back to digits\n    new_left_part_digits = _int_to_digits(left_part_int)\n\n    # Check if incrementing the left part increased its digit count\n    # This happens when the left part consisted entirely of 9s (e.g., 9 -> 10, 99 -> 100)\n    if len(new_left_part_digits) > half_len:\n        # If the length of new_left_part_digits is greater than half_len,\n        # it means the number was something like 9, 99, 999 etc.\n        # The resulting palindrome will have num_digits + 1 digits, e.g.,\n        # 9 -> 11, 99 -> 101, 999 -> 1001.\n        # This can be constructed as '1' followed by 'num_digits - 1' zeros, then '1'.\n        result_digits = [1] + [0] * (num_digits - 1) + [1]\n        return _digits_to_int(result_digits)\n    else:\n        # The length of the left part did not change after incrementing.\n        # Construct the final palindrome by mirroring the new_left_part_digits.\n        final_palindrome_digits = new_left_part_digits + new_left_part_digits[:num_digits // 2][::-1]\n        return _digits_to_int(final_palindrome_digits)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/102", "solution": "```python\ndef snake_to_camel(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a snake_case string to a PascalCase (CamelCase) string.\n    Multiple underscores and leading/trailing underscores are handled by removing empty segments.\n    \"\"\"\n    if s == \"\":\n        return \"\"\n    \n    parts = s.split('_')\n    camel_parts = []\n    for part in parts:\n        if part:  # Ignore empty parts from multiple underscores or leading/trailing\n            camel_parts.append(part[0].upper() + part[1:])\n    return \"\".join(camel_parts)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/103", "solution": "import collections\n\ndef eulerian_num(n: int, m: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the Eulerian number a(n, m) using dynamic programming.\n    This implementation uses a memory-optimized approach, keeping only the previous row.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1 if m == 0 else 0\n    if m < 0 or m >= n:\n        return 0\n\n    # Initialize the first row for n=0\n    # prev[k] corresponds to a(level-1, k)\n    prev = collections.deque([0] * n) # Represents previous row, for n=0, it's a(0,0)=1\n    prev.appendleft(1) # effectively prev = [1, 0, ..., 0] for n-1 slots after (n=0)\n\n    # Iterate from level 1 up to n\n    for level in range(1, n + 1):\n        # curr will store a(level, k) values\n        # The size of curr will be 'level' (for k from 0 to level-1)\n        curr = collections.deque([0] * level)\n\n        # Calculate a(level, k) using the recurrence relation\n        # a(level, k) = (level - k) * a(level-1, k-1) + (k + 1) * a(level-1, k)\n        for k in range(level):\n            val = 0\n            # Term (level - k) * a(level-1, k-1)\n            # a(level-1, k-1) is prev[k-1]\n            if k - 1 >= 0 and k - 1 < len(prev):\n                val += (level - k) * prev[k - 1]\n            \n            # Term (k + 1) * a(level-1, k)\n            # a(level-1, k) is prev[k]\n            if k < len(prev):\n                val += (k + 1) * prev[k]\n            \n            curr[k] = val\n        prev = curr\n    \n    return prev[m]"}
