{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef find_kth(a: List[int], b: List[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the k-th smallest element from two sorted arrays using a binary search approach.\n    Assumes `a` and `b` are sorted lists of integers, and `k` is a 1-based positive integer\n    within the total length of the two arrays.\n    \"\"\"\n    nums1, nums2 = a, b\n    m, n = len(nums1), len(nums2)\n\n    # Ensure nums1 is the smaller array to optimize the binary search range.\n    if m > n:\n        nums1, nums2 = nums2, nums1\n        m, n = n, m\n\n    # Define the search range for the partition point in nums1 (partition_x).\n    # partition_x is the number of elements taken from nums1.\n    # partition_y is the number of elements taken from nums2 (k - partition_x).\n    # Both partition_x and partition_y must be valid indices (0 to length).\n    low = max(0, k - n)  # Minimum possible partition_x to ensure partition_y <= n\n    high = min(k, m)     # Maximum possible partition_x to ensure partition_x <= m and partition_y >= 0\n\n    while low <= high:\n        partition_x = low + (high - low) // 2\n        partition_y = k - partition_x\n\n        # Determine the elements at the partition boundaries.\n        # Use float('-inf') if a partition is empty (no elements taken from that side).\n        # Use float('inf') if a partition includes all elements (no elements left on the other side).\n        left_x = nums1[partition_x - 1] if partition_x > 0 else float('-inf')\n        right_x = nums1[partition_x] if partition_x < m else float('inf')\n\n        left_y = nums2[partition_y - 1] if partition_y > 0 else float('-inf')\n        right_y = nums2[partition_y] if partition_y < n else float('inf')\n\n        # Check if the partition is valid:\n        # All elements in the left halves must be less than or equal to all elements in the right halves.\n        if left_x <= right_y and left_y <= right_x:\n            # Correct partition found. The k-th element is the largest among the left halves.\n            return max(left_x, left_y)\n        elif left_x > right_y:\n            # partition_x is too large; too many elements are taken from nums1.\n            # Adjust the high boundary to search in the left half of the current range.\n            high = partition_x - 1\n        else:  # left_y > right_x\n            # partition_x is too small; too many elements are taken from nums2.\n            # Adjust the low boundary to search in the right half of the current range.\n            low = partition_x + 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "def armstrong_number(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a number is an Armstrong number.\n    An Armstrong number is one whose sum of digits raised to the power of the number of digits is equal to the number itself.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 0:\n        return False\n\n    # Calculate the number of digits\n    # For a number like 0, str(0) is '0', len('0') is 1. This handles 0 correctly as 0^1 = 0.\n    num_str = str(number)\n    num_digits = len(num_str)\n\n    sum_of_powers = 0\n    for digit_char in num_str:\n        digit = int(digit_char)\n        sum_of_powers += digit ** num_digits\n\n    return sum_of_powers == number"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/594", "solution": "def diff_even_odd(numbers: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the difference of the first even and first odd number in a list.\n    Assumes valid input will lead to both an even and an odd number being found.\n    \"\"\"\n    first_even = None\n    first_odd = None\n\n    for item in numbers:\n        # Attempt to convert the item to an integer. If conversion fails,\n        # Python's ValueError will propagate, as per \"DO NOT raise exceptions for edge cases\".\n        num = int(item)\n\n        if first_even is None and num % 2 == 0:\n            first_even = num\n        \n        if first_odd is None and num % 2 != 0:\n            first_odd = num\n        \n        # Optimization: stop iteration once both numbers are found\n        if first_even is not None and first_odd is not None:\n            break\n    \n    # If either first_even or first_odd is still None, the subtraction will\n    # implicitly raise a TypeError, consistent with \"DO NOT raise exceptions for edge cases\".\n    return first_even - first_odd"}
